================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING REACT'S STATE SYSTEM
================================================================================

HOW A REACT STATE UPDATE WORKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A typical React state update follows this flow:

1. Component Renders
        ↓
2. React Displays UI
        ↓
3. User Interacts
        ↓
4. Event Handler Runs
        ↓
5. State Setter Is Called
        ↓
6. React Processes the State Update
        ↓
7. Component Re-renders
        ↓
8. React Reconciles the New Output
        ↓
9. Required DOM Changes Are Applied
        ↓
10. User Sees Updated UI


THE PROBLEM WITH DIRECT MUTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When state is mutated directly:

• React's state setter is bypassed

• The existing object/array may keep the same reference

• React may have no scheduled state update for that mutation

• The component may not re-render because of that mutation

• The displayed UI can become inconsistent with mutated data

• Bugs become difficult to understand

• Shared references can introduce unintended side effects


STATE IMMUTABILITY PRINCIPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State should be treated as immutable.

Instead of modifying the existing state value:

Existing State
      ↓
   MUTATE
      ↓
Same Object/Array


Prefer:

Existing State
      ↓
Create Updated Value
      ↓
Call State Setter
      ↓
React Processes Update
      ↓
Re-render


WHY IMMUTABILITY MATTERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| REASON               | BENEFIT                                       |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| Change Tracking      | New references make updates easier to track   |
| Predictability       | Previous state isn't unexpectedly modified    |
| Optimization         | Supports shallow/reference comparisons        |
| Debugging            | State transitions are easier to reason about  |
| Testing              | Previous and next values remain distinct      |
| Reliability          | Reduces mutation-related side effects         |
| Collaboration        | Establishes predictable coding patterns       |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 3: THE WRONG WAY - DIRECT MUTATION
================================================================================

UNDERSTANDING DIRECT MUTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Direct mutation means:

• Changing state object properties directly

• Modifying state arrays in place

• Changing nested objects directly

• Reusing the same mutable object/array

• Bypassing the state setter function

• Creating state/UI synchronization problems


================================================================================
WRONG WAY EXAMPLE 1: CHANGING A SIMPLE STATE VALUE DIRECTLY
================================================================================

IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The original example attempted:

count = count + 1

However, because count comes from:

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

assigning directly to count is invalid because count is declared using const.

Therefore, the example below shows the conceptual wrong approach while
keeping the JavaScript behavior technically clear.


WRONG CONCEPT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


  // ❌ WRONG IDEA:
  // Trying to modify state without setCount()

  const handleIncrement = () => {

    // count = count + 1

    // Wrong:
    // count is a const binding and state must be updated
    // through setCount()
  }


  // ❌ WRONG:
  // Changing only a separate local variable does not update state

  const handleDecrement = () => {

    let newCount = count

    newCount = newCount - 1

    // newCount changed,
    // but React state "count" did not change
  }


  return (
    <div className="counter">

      <p>
        Count: {count}
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleDecrement}>
        Decrement
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


WHY THIS IS WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• State Setter Is Not Used
  - setCount() is never called

• Local Variables Are Not State
  - Changing newCount does not update count

• No State Update Is Scheduled
  - React has not been asked to update count

• UI Does Not Reflect the Intended Change
  - count remains the state value previously stored by React


LINE-BY-LINE EXPLANATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+--------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
| CODE / CONCEPT                       | EXPLANATION                           |
+--------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
| const [count, setCount]              | Creates count state correctly         |
| useState(0)                          | Initializes count to 0                |
| count = count + 1                    | Invalid direct reassignment           |
| let newCount = count                 | Copies primitive value locally        |
| newCount = newCount - 1              | Changes local variable only           |
| setCount() not called                | React state remains unchanged         |
| Count: {count}                       | Displays actual React state           |
+--------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+


CORRECT CONCEPT PREVIEW:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setCount(count + 1)

setCount(count - 1)


