WRONG WAY EXAMPLE 2: MUTATING OBJECT STATE
================================================================================

The following code demonstrates the wrong way to update an object stored
in state:


import { useState } from 'react'


export default function UserProfile() {

  const [user, setUser] = useState({
    name: 'John Doe',
    email: 'john@example.com',
    age: 30
  })


  // ❌ WRONG - Mutating object directly

  const handleNameChange = (newName) => {

    user.name = newName

    // Existing user object is mutated
    // setUser() is not called
  }


  // ❌ WRONG - Mutating object directly

  const handleAgeChange = (newAge) => {

    user.age = newAge

    // Existing user object is mutated
    // setUser() is not called
  }


  return (
    <div className="profile">

      <h2>{user.name}</h2>

      <p>
        Email: {user.email}
      </p>

      <p>
        Age: {user.age}
      </p>

      <input
        onChange={(e) =>
          handleNameChange(e.target.value)
        }
        placeholder="Enter name"
      />

      <input
        onChange={(e) =>
          handleAgeChange(e.target.value)
        }
        placeholder="Enter age"
      />

    </div>
  )
}


WHY THIS IS WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• user.name = newName
  - Directly modifies the existing state object

• user.age = newAge
  - Directly modifies the existing state object

• setUser() Is Not Called
  - React is not explicitly notified of a state update

• Same Object Is Mutated
  - No new user object is created

• UI May Remain Stale
  - The mutation itself does not schedule a render


LINE-BY-LINE EXPLANATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| CODE LINE                        | WHY IT'S WRONG                            |
+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| useState({...})                  | Correctly creates user state              |
| user.name = newName              | Mutates state object directly            |
| user.age = newAge                | Mutates state object directly            |
| setUser() not called             | No React state update requested          |
| {user.name}                      | Reads current object during rendering     |
| {user.age}                       | Reads current object during rendering     |
+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+


CORRECT PATTERN PREVIEW:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setUser({
  ...user,
  name: newName
})


setUser({
  ...user,
  age: newAge
})


================================================================================
WRONG WAY EXAMPLE 3: MUTATING ARRAYS
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function TodoList() {

  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([
    {
      id: 1,
      text: 'Learn React',
      completed: false
    },
    {
      id: 2,
      text: 'Build a project',
      completed: false
    }
  ])


  // ❌ WRONG - push() mutates existing array

  const handleAddTodo = (newTodo) => {

    todos.push(newTodo)

    // Same todos array is modified
    // setTodos() is not called
  }


  // ❌ WRONG - splice() mutates existing array

  const handleRemoveTodo = (id) => {

    const index =
      todos.findIndex(todo => todo.id === id)

    todos.splice(index, 1)

    // Existing array is mutated
  }


  // ❌ WRONG - Object inside state array is mutated

  const handleToggleTodo = (id) => {

    const todo =
      todos.find(todo => todo.id === id)

    todo.completed = !todo.completed

    // Existing todo object is mutated
  }


  return (
    <div className="todo-list">

      <h2>My Todos</h2>

      <ul>

        {todos.map(todo => (

          <li key={todo.id}>

            <input
              type="checkbox"
              checked={todo.completed}
              onChange={() =>
                handleToggleTodo(todo.id)
              }
            />

            <span>
              {todo.text}
            </span>

            <button
              onClick={() =>
                handleRemoveTodo(todo.id)
              }
            >
              Delete
            </button>

          </li>

        ))}

      </ul>


      <button
        onClick={() =>
          handleAddTodo({
            id: 3,
            text: 'New Todo',
            completed: false
          })
        }
      >
        Add Todo
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


WHY THIS IS WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• todos.push(newTodo)
  - Mutates the existing array

• todos.splice(index, 1)
  - Mutates the existing array

• todo.completed = ...
  - Mutates an existing object inside the array

• setTodos() Is Not Called
  - No state update is explicitly requested

• Existing References Are Reused
  - Array/items are modified instead of replaced


MUTATING ARRAY METHODS TO WATCH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------+----------------------------------------------+
| METHOD         | EFFECT                                       |
+----------------+----------------------------------------------+
| push()         | Adds item to existing array                  |
| pop()          | Removes last item from existing array        |
| splice()       | Adds/removes items in existing array         |
| shift()        | Removes first item                           |
| unshift()      | Adds item to beginning                       |
| sort()         | Sorts existing array in place                |
| reverse()      | Reverses existing array in place             |
+----------------+----------------------------------------------+


COMMON IMMUTABLE ALTERNATIVES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------+
| REQUIREMENT           | COMMON APPROACH                             |
+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------+
| Add item              | [...array, item]                            |
| Remove item           | array.filter(...)                           |
| Update item           | array.map(...)                              |
| Copy array            | [...array]                                  |
| Sort without mutation | [...array].sort(...)                        |
| Reverse without       | [...array].reverse()                        |
| modifying original    |                                             |
+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------+


