PART 4: CONSEQUENCES OF WRONG STATE UPDATES
================================================================================

WHAT CAN HAPPEN WHEN YOU MUTATE STATE?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• No Update Scheduled
  - If no state setter is called, mutation itself does not ask React
    to render again

• UI Can Become Out of Sync
  - The visible interface may not reflect mutated values

• Bugs Can Appear Later
  - Another unrelated render may suddenly expose the mutated value

• Debugging Becomes Difficult
  - Memory can contain a modified object while the displayed UI remains old

• Previous State Is Lost
  - Mutation changes an existing object rather than preserving its old value

• Optimization Can Break
  - Reference-based optimization becomes unreliable

• Behavior Becomes Harder to Predict
  - Different parts of the application may share the same mutated reference


CONSEQUENCES TABLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------------+------------------------------------------+
| ISSUE                      | CONSEQUENCE                              |
+----------------------------+------------------------------------------+
| State setter not called    | No state update is scheduled            |
| Existing object mutated    | Previous object contents are modified   |
| Same reference reused      | Reference checks can't show replacement |
| No immediate re-render     | UI may remain outdated                  |
| UI/data mismatch           | Confusing user experience               |
| Silent mutation            | Debugging becomes harder                |
| Shared reference mutation  | Other consumers may see side effects    |
| Poor change history        | Harder to track previous/next state     |
+----------------------------+------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE: BUG DEMONSTRATION
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function BugDemo() {

  const [user, setUser] = useState({
    name: 'John',
    email: 'john@example.com'
  })


  // ❌ WRONG - Mutates state directly

  const handleUpdateName = (newName) => {

    user.name = newName

    console.log('Mutated user object:', user)

    // The JavaScript object was mutated
    // but setUser() was not called
  }


  return (
    <div>

      <h2>
        User: {user.name}
      </h2>

      <p>
        Email: {user.email}
      </p>

      <input
        type="text"
        onChange={(e) =>
          handleUpdateName(e.target.value)
        }
        placeholder="Enter new name"
      />

      <p>
        The JavaScript object can show the mutated value
        in the console even though that mutation did not
        schedule a React state update.
      </p>

    </div>
  )
}


WHAT HAPPENS?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. User types "John Smith"

        ↓

2. handleUpdateName("John Smith") runs

        ↓

3. Existing user object is mutated

   user.name = 'John Smith'

        ↓

4. console.log() can display the mutated object

        ↓

5. setUser() was NOT called

        ↓

6. That mutation itself does not schedule a React re-render

        ↓

7. Display can remain:

   User: John


WHY THE CONSOLE CAN SHOW THE MUTATED VALUE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• JavaScript Object Was Mutated
  - user.name actually changed on the existing object

• Console Can Display the Object's Current Contents
  - The object may show the changed property

• React State Update Was Not Requested
  - setUser() was never called

• The Two Ideas Are Different

  JavaScript object mutation
              ≠
  React state update


IMPORTANT MEMORY POINT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"Object changed in memory"

does NOT automatically mean:

"React processed a state update and re-rendered the component"


================================================================================
PART 5: COMMON MUTATION PATTERNS TO AVOID
================================================================================

PATTERN 1: DIRECT PROPERTY ASSIGNMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ❌ WRONG

state.property = newValue


Why it's wrong:

• Mutates an existing state object
• Does not create a replacement object
• Bypasses the state setter


CORRECT IDEA:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setState({
  ...state,
  property: newValue
})


================================================================================
PATTERN 2: MUTATING ARRAY METHODS
================================================================================

// ❌ WRONG

array.push(newItem)

array.pop()

array.splice(index, 1)

array.shift()

array.unshift(newItem)

array.sort()

array.reverse()


Why these are wrong when applied directly to state arrays:

• They modify the existing array in place

• Existing state reference is mutated

• Previous array contents are not preserved as a separate state value


IMMUTABLE ALTERNATIVES:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Add

setArray([
  ...array,
  newItem
])


// Remove

setArray(
  array.filter(item => item.id !== id)
)


// Update

setArray(
  array.map(item =>
    item.id === id
      ? { ...item, property: newValue }
      : item
  )
)


// Sort

setArray(
  [...array].sort(compareFunction)
)


// Reverse

setArray(
  [...array].reverse()
)


================================================================================
PATTERN 3: NESTED OBJECT MUTATION
================================================================================

// ❌ WRONG

state.nested.property = newValue


Why it's wrong:

• Mutates an existing nested object
• Existing nested reference is modified
• Makes state changes harder to reason about


CORRECT IDEA:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setState({
  ...state,

  nested: {
    ...state.nested,
    property: newValue
  }
})


================================================================================
PATTERN 4: CONDITIONAL MUTATION
================================================================================

// ❌ WRONG

const handleToggle = () => {

  if (condition) {
    state.property = newValue
  }
}


Why it's wrong:

• Still directly mutates state

• Conditional logic does not make mutation safe

• Makes the mutation harder to notice


CORRECT IDEA:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const handleToggle = () => {

  if (condition) {

    setState({
      ...state,
      property: newValue
    })
  }
}


