PART 6: WHY REACT REQUIRES IMMUTABILITY
================================================================================

REFERENCE IDENTITY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Objects and arrays are reference values.

Example:

const object1 = {
  count: 0
}

const object2 = object1


Here:

object1 === object2

Result:

true


Both variables refer to the SAME object.


MUTATION:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const state = {
  count: 0
}

const sameObject = state

sameObject.count = 1


The object was changed, but:

state === sameObject

is still:

true


IMMUTABLE REPLACEMENT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const state = {
  count: 0
}


const newState = {
  ...state,
  count: 1
}


Now:

state === newState

is:

false


The updated object has a different reference.


REFERENCE VS VALUE EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const state = {
  count: 1
}


const newState = {
  count: 1
}


The objects contain equivalent values, but:

state === newState

is:

false


Why?

Because they are two different objects.


WHY IMMUTABILITY MATTERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Preserves Previous Values
  - Existing objects aren't modified

• Makes Updates Explicit
  - New values are passed through state setters

• Supports Reference Comparisons
  - Previous and next object references can differ

• Improves Predictability
  - State has clearer before/after values

• Simplifies Debugging
  - State transitions are easier to inspect

• Supports Optimization
  - Reference equality can be used by React optimization mechanisms

• Works with State Management Patterns
  - Immutable update patterns are widely used in React state management


IMPORTANT TECHNICAL NOTE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Avoid oversimplifying React state as:

"Different reference = always re-render"
"Same reference = never re-render"

A better mental model is:

1. Treat state as immutable
2. Create the next state value
3. Pass it to the state setter
4. React determines how to process that update

For state setter updates, React can skip work when the next state is
equivalent to the current state according to its state equality behavior.

The practical rule remains:

NEVER mutate existing state directly.


