PART 7: IDENTIFYING MUTATION BUGS
================================================================================

SIGNS OF STATE MUTATION BUGS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Watch for:

• UI Doesn't Update as Expected

• Object Appears Changed in Console but UI Doesn't Reflect It

• Changes Appear During an Unrelated Later Render

• State and Display Become Inconsistent

• Behavior Is Difficult to Reproduce

• Nested Values Change Unexpectedly

• Shared Objects Show Unexpected Side Effects

• Memoized Components Don't Update as Expected


DEBUGGING MUTATION ISSUES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. CHECK STATE SETTER CALLS

   Ask:

   "Am I calling the appropriate setter?"


2. LOOK FOR DIRECT ASSIGNMENTS

   Search for patterns such as:

   state.property = value


3. CHECK ARRAY MUTATIONS

   Look for:

   push()
   pop()
   splice()
   shift()
   unshift()
   sort()
   reverse()


4. CHECK NESTED OBJECT MUTATIONS

   Example:

   state.user.address.city = 'Mumbai'


5. CHECK OBJECT/ARRAY REFERENCES

   Ask:

   "Did I create a new object or array?"


6. USE REACT DEVTOOLS

   Inspect component state and props.


7. TRACE EVENT HANDLERS

   Verify:

   User Event
       ↓
   Handler
       ↓
   State Setter
       ↓
   State Update
       ↓
   Render


================================================================================
COMMON DEBUG PATTERNS
================================================================================

PATTERN 1: LOG THE PREVIOUS VALUE AND REQUESTED NEXT VALUE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const handleUpdate = (newValue) => {

  console.log('Current:', state)

  console.log('Requested Next:', newValue)

  setState(newValue)
}


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Do not expect this pattern:

setState(newValue)
console.log(state)

to immediately show the newly rendered state value inside the same
running event-handler closure.

The current handler uses the state value from the render in which that
handler was created.


PATTERN 2: CHECK REFERENCES BEFORE THE UPDATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const newState = {
  ...state,
  property: newValue
}


console.log(
  'Same reference?',
  state === newState
)

// Expected:
// false


PATTERN 3: USE REACT DEVTOOLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use the React DevTools Components view to inspect component state and
props while debugging.


================================================================================
PART 8: COMPARISON TABLE - WRONG VS RIGHT
================================================================================

WRONG WAY VS RIGHT WAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+-----------------------+----------------------------+--------------------------------+
| SCENARIO              | WRONG WAY                  | RIGHT WAY                      |
+-----------------------+----------------------------+--------------------------------+
| Simple value          | Direct reassignment        | setState(newValue)             |
| Object property       | state.prop = value         | setState({...state,            |
|                       |                            |   prop: value})                 |
| Add array item        | array.push(item)           | setState([...array, item])      |
| Remove array item     | array.splice(i, 1)         | setState(array.filter(...))     |
| Update array item     | array[i].prop = value      | setState(array.map(...))        |
| Nested object         | state.nested.prop = value  | Copy outer + nested objects     |
| Toggle boolean        | state.flag = !state.flag   | setState({...state,             |
|                       |                            |   flag: !state.flag})           |
| Increment object      | state.count++              | setState({...state,             |
| property              |                            |   count: state.count + 1})      |
| Sort array            | array.sort()               | setState([...array].sort())     |
| Reverse array         | array.reverse()            | setState([...array].reverse())  |
+-----------------------+----------------------------+--------------------------------+


FOR SIMPLE useState VALUE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


// ✓ Correct

setCount(count + 1)


For updates derived from previous state, the functional form is often
even safer:

setCount(previousCount => previousCount + 1)


================================================================================
PART 9: SUMMARY OF WRONG PATTERNS
================================================================================

KEY TAKEAWAYS ABOUT WRONG STATE UPDATES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Remember:

• Never mutate state directly

• Use the state setter to request updates

• Don't modify objects stored in state directly

• Don't modify arrays stored in state directly

• Create updated objects and arrays

• Copy nested objects when updating nested properties

• Don't assume console output means React performed an update

• Avoid reusing a mutated state object as the intended new state

• Keep state transitions explicit and predictable


COMMON MISTAKES SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------------+------------------------------------------+
| MISTAKE                    | PROBLEM                                  |
+----------------------------+------------------------------------------+
| Forget setter              | No React state update requested         |
| state.prop = value         | Direct object mutation                   |
| array.push()               | Existing array modified                 |
| array.pop()                | Existing array modified                 |
| array.splice()             | Existing array modified                 |
| array.sort()               | Existing array reordered in place       |
| array.reverse()            | Existing array reversed in place        |
| nested.prop = value        | Nested state mutation                   |
| Reuse mutated reference    | Previous state object is modified       |
| Trust console alone        | Doesn't prove a React update occurred   |
+----------------------------+------------------------------------------+


NOTE ABOUT THE key PROP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Using a correct key is important when rendering lists, but it is a
different concern from state immutability.

Example:

{todos.map(todo => (
  <Todo
    key={todo.id}
    todo={todo}
  />
))}

key helps React identify list elements across renders.

It does NOT make direct state mutation safe.


