PREVIEW OF CORRECT PATTERNS
================================================================================

1. UPDATE SIMPLE VALUE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setState(newValue)


2. UPDATE VALUE USING PREVIOUS STATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setCount(previousCount =>
  previousCount + 1
)


3. UPDATE OBJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setState({
  ...state,
  property: newValue
})


4. ADD TO ARRAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setArray([
  ...array,
  newItem
])


5. REMOVE FROM ARRAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setArray(
  array.filter(
    item => item.id !== idToRemove
  )
)


6. UPDATE ARRAY ITEM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setArray(
  array.map(item =>

    item.id === idToUpdate
      ? {
          ...item,
          property: newValue
        }
      : item

  )
)


7. UPDATE NESTED OBJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setState({
  ...state,

  nested: {
    ...state.nested,
    property: newValue
  }
})


WHY THESE PATTERNS WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• State Setter Is Used
  - React receives an update request

• Existing State Isn't Mutated
  - Previous values remain untouched

• New Objects/Arrays Are Created Where Required
  - References represent new versions of state

• Component Can Render the Updated State

• State Transitions Are Easier to Debug

• Updates Are More Predictable

• Patterns Work Well with React's optimization mechanisms


